{
  "requirement": "`matches` holds the results found. `complete` is true when the search exhausted the scope. Reaching the exact limit is compatible with a complete result if no further match exists.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct SearchResults<T> {\n    pub matches: Vec<T>,\n    pub complete: bool,\n}\n\nimpl<T> SearchResults<T> {\n    pub fn new(matches: Vec<T>, complete: bool) -> Self;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations derived from `requirements.sdk.graph.search.SearchResults`**\n1. `matches` holds the results found.\n2. `complete` is true when the search exhausted the scope.\n3. Reaching the exact limit is still complete if no further match exists. A limit that omits a match is incomplete.\n4. The type has the public shape `#[non_exhaustive] struct SearchResults<T> { matches, complete }` and a public `new(matches, complete)`.\n\n**Test mapping**\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs#fn[completeness_is_false_only_when_a_match_is_omitted_and_errors_propagate]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It drives `search_text` through the `Memory` provider, which has two hits, with these cases:\n\n| limit | matches | complete |\n|---|---|---|\n| none | 2 | true |\n| 2 (exact limit, nothing further) | 2 | true |\n| 1 (a match omitted) | 1 | false |\n| 0 (matches exist) | 0 | false |\n\n  It asserts both `result.matches.len()` and `result.complete` for each case. The test also covers limit 0 with a pattern that matches nothing, which gives an empty `matches` and `complete == true`. This covers the exact-limit boundary and the omitted-match case. Error cases (a bad regex, bad bytes, an unknown language) are asserted to return errors rather than results.\n- Structural search is not marked for the subject but is covered under `StructuralMatches`, which is the same generic type.\n  - `structural_search_uses_overridden_parses_their_snapshot_and_requested_scope` checks limit None, `Some(1)` and `Some(0)` against `(count, complete)`.\n  - `built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope` in `tests/source_defaults.rs` asserts `(len, complete)` per limit, and asserts `!subset.complete` when the limit is 1 and two matches exist.\n  - Together they show the type's semantics hold for the second instantiation.\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` is marked for the subject. It generates rustdoc and compares the export inventory against the specification, so the public type's presence and kind are checked.\n\n**Exemption and gaps**\n- `SearchResults::new` is never called by a test. It is a pure pass-through, `Self { matches, complete }`, with no conditional or transformation, and the requirement does not state an unchanged-value obligation for it. The exemption therefore applies.\n- I found no gap: the completeness semantics, including the exact-limit boundary, are tested with assertions on both fields, and the public shape is checked by the API inventory test.",
  "references": "b2822f2e9b6c650a588222facdf23a70a73eea6f3c81aad00bc855db6d78b89f",
